  "content": "<p><strong>Unit 19 - Triangles — Title &amp; Goal</strong><br />Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 19 - Triangles for SAT Math preparation.</p>\n\n\n<h3>Key idea</h3>\n<p>Read the idea and scan the rules. Focus on how the symbols behave.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Isosceles Triangle</strong>: 2 equal sides → 2 equal angles.  </li>\n<li><strong>Equilateral Triangle</strong>: All sides equal, all angles <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Worked example</h3>\n<p>Follow the steps carefully, then try to reproduce them without looking.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 1</strong><br/>\nIf an isosceles triangle has vertex angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>, each base angle is:  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>\n<h3>Quick practice</h3>\n<p>Try these on your own. Check your steps, not just the final answer.</p>\n<p>1) Find the missing angle in a triangle with angles <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.\n2) In an isosceles triangle, the vertex angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Find the base angles.\n3) A right triangle has legs 9 and 12. Find the hypotenuse.\n<h3>Next idea</h3>\n<p>Build on the previous idea. Notice what changes and what stays the same.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p>A <strong>right triangle</strong> has one <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> angle.<br/>\nPythagorean theorem:</p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> is the hypotenuse.  </p>\n<p>Common triples: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr/>\n<h3>45-45-90 Triangle</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Legs equal.  </li>\n<li>Hypotenuse <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>leg</mtext><mi>·</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>30-60-90 Triangle</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Short leg <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.  </li>\n<li>Hypotenuse <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.  </li>\n<li>Long leg <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Another example</h3>\n<p>Compare this approach to the previous one. What’s the same? What’s different?</p>\n<p><strong>Example 2</strong><br/>\nIn a 30-60-90 triangle, if the hypotenuse is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, then:<br/>\n- Short leg <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math><br/>\n- Long leg <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> </p>\n<hr/>\n<p>Triangles are <strong>similar</strong> if their angles are equal and sides proportional.  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\n<p><strong>Example 3</strong><br/>\nIf <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>△</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>~</mi><mi>△</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>, find <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>Scale factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.<br/>\nSo <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>·</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>·</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr/>\n<p>Radians measure angles in terms of arc length.  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></math>\n<p>Conversions:<br/>\n- Degrees <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>→</mo></mrow></math> radians: multiply by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>180</mn></mrow></math><br/>\n- Radians <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>→</mo></mrow></math> degrees: multiply by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></math> </p>\n<p><strong>Example 4</strong><br/>\nConvert <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> to radians:  </p>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>·</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>180</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\n<hr/>\n<ul>\n<li>Always check if triangle is special (30-60-90 or 45-45-90).  </li>\n<li>Use Pythagorean triples to save time.  </li>\n<li>Mark equal sides/angles in isosceles problems.  </li>\n<li>Watch for hidden similar triangles (especially in geometry diagrams).  </li>\n</ul>\n<hr/>\n<p>1) Find the missing angle in a triangle with angles <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.<br/>\n2) In an isosceles triangle, the vertex angle is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Find the base angles.<br/>\n3) A right triangle has legs 9 and 12. Find the hypotenuse.<br/>\n4) In a 45-45-90 triangle, the hypotenuse is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. Find the legs.<br/>\n5) Convert <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>225</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> into radians.<br/>\n6) Two similar triangles have sides <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Find <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr/>\n<p>1) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>75</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>65</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>2) Base angles <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>3) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>12</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>81</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>144</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>225</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>4) Leg <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>5) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>225</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi>·</mi><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>6) Scale factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<h3>More practice</h3>\n<p>Keep going—aim for accuracy first, then speed.</p>\n<h3>Mixed practice set</h3>\n<p>These mix everything above. Time yourself and reflect on any mistakes.</p>\n4) In a 45-45-90 triangle, the hypotenuse is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. Find the legs.\n5) Convert <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mn>225</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math> into radians.\n6) Two similar triangles have sides <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Find <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.",
